  select $astroPT_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "How does the deferent-and-epicycle system produce retrograde motion with the Earth at rest?", "options": ["The planet physically stops on its circle once per orbit", "A large DEFERENT circle is centered near the Earth; a small EPICYCLE rides around on the deferent; the planet rides the epicycle. The planet's motion is the SUM of two circular motions \u2014 and when it swings through the NEAR side of its epicycle, its epicyclic motion runs opposite to the deferent's, so (if fast enough) it appears from Earth to move BACKWARD: a retrograde loop, manufactured from uniform circles", "The Sun's gravity pulls the planet backward at opposition", "The deferent reverses direction periodically to create the loop"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The deferent is a large circle centered near the Earth; the epicycle is a small circle whose center rides around the deferent; the planet rides the epicycle. Adding the two uniform circular motions, the planet traces a looping path \u2014 mostly forward, but with backward loops each time it swings through the near side of its epicycle, where its epicyclic motion runs opposite to the deferent's carrying motion. Seen from the fixed Earth, that is retrograde motion, built entirely from perfect circles. It is the elegant geometric answer to the central puzzle."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "What was the equant, and why was it both brilliant and controversial?", "options": ["The point where the planet is closest to Earth; it was uncontroversial", "A special point offset from the deferent's center (opposite the Earth) about which the epicycle's center moves at UNIFORM ANGULAR SPEED \u2014 brilliant because it made predictions remarkably accurate, but controversial because the motion is then uniform only as seen from that arbitrary point, NOT truly uniform around the circle's center, quietly violating the 'circle dogma' of genuine uniform circular motion", "Another name for the deferent's center", "The device that offset the Earth from the center (that was actually the eccentric)"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The equant is a point offset from the deferent's center, on the opposite side from the Earth, about which Ptolemy decreed the epicycle's center sweeps equal angles in equal times. It was brilliant because it fit the data superbly and made the Almagest's predictions accurate for centuries. But it was a subtle betrayal of the circle dogma: the motion is uniform only as seen from the equant \u2014 neither the center nor the Earth \u2014 so the planet does not truly move at constant speed around its circle. Purists found it a blemish, and Copernicus would cite his wish to banish it as a prime motive for moving the Earth. (The device that offset the EARTH was the separate 'eccentric.')"}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why did Ptolemy's system endure for roughly fourteen centuries \u2014 the longest-lived scientific theory in history?", "options": ["Because the Church forbade anyone from questioning it for 1,400 years", "Above all because it WORKED \u2014 it successfully PREDICTED the positions of the planets, Sun, and Moon well enough for practical purposes, century after century; it also matched the strong evidence for a stationary Earth and meshed with Aristotelian physics into a single coherent worldview (religious authority was added only later)", "Because no one did any astronomy at all during that period", "Because it was mathematically simpler than any alternative"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The root of its success was scientific: it predicted the sky. A theory that successfully predicts is a good theory, and Ptolemy's did so for practical purposes for over a millennium. It also fit the powerful evidence for a motionless Earth and meshed with Aristotelian physics (the changeable sublunary realm vs. the perfect celestial realm), forming a coherent, satisfying, evidence-supported picture. Its later entanglement with Christian and Islamic theology added authority, but the foundation was empirical success \u2014 which is exactly why overturning it was so monumental."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "What role did the Islamic Golden Age play between Ptolemy and Copernicus?", "options": ["None \u2014 astronomy was frozen until Copernicus revived it directly from Ptolemy", "It preserved AND ADVANCED Greek astronomy for centuries: translating the texts (we know Ptolemy's book by its Arabic name, Almagest), correcting its numbers (al-Battani, al-Sufi), and attacking its flaws \u2014 the Maragha school and Ibn al-Shatir built equant-free models using devices (like the Tusi couple) that reappear almost identically in Copernicus, who thus inherited centuries of Islamic refinement", "It rejected Ptolemy entirely and invented heliocentrism first", "It only copied the texts without understanding or improving them"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "After Greek science was largely lost to Western Europe, it survived and advanced in the Islamic world \u2014 Baghdad, Cairo, C\u00f3rdoba, Samarkand, Damascus, Maragha. This was living science: al-Battani improved Ptolemy's constants, al-Sufi cataloged the stars (and first recorded Andromeda), and the Maragha school and Ibn al-Shatir built sophisticated equant-free planetary models. Devices equivalent to al-Tusi's 'Tusi couple' \u2014 and models like Ibn al-Shatir's \u2014 reappear almost identically in Copernicus, so most historians conclude he inherited them. Copernicus did not build the revolution from Ptolemy alone; the very name 'Almagest,' and the Arabic names of the bright stars, record this thousand-year bridge."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "What were the two great 'unexplained coincidences' in Ptolemy's system, and why did they matter?", "options": ["That the planets are round and that they shine \u2014 both obvious", "(i) Mercury and Venus NEVER stray far from the Sun (always morning/evening stars), and (ii) retrograde ALWAYS happens at opposition (outer planets) \u2014 both had to be imposed BY HAND, tying the epicycles to the Sun's motion for no reason the model provided. They mattered because they become AUTOMATIC consequences of heliocentrism \u2014 so they were secretly 'fingerprints' of the moving-Earth system to come", "That the Sun and Moon are the same size in the sky; it meant nothing", "That eclipses happen; Ptolemy could not predict them"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Two coincidences the model could reproduce but not explain: Mercury and Venus are always seen near the Sun (their epicycle centers had to be arbitrarily tied to the Sun's direction), and outer-planet retrograde always occurs at opposition (their epicycles had to be synchronized with the Sun by hand). Why should every planet's motion be chained to the Sun? Geocentrism had no answer. But both fall out FOR FREE in heliocentrism: Mercury and Venus have orbits inside Earth's, so they cannot appear far from the Sun; and retrograde must occur at opposition, when Earth overtakes an outer planet. The strains the old system imposed by hand were fingerprints of the new structure."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Explain the machinery of Ptolemy's Almagest: the deferent-and-epicycle system and how it manufactures retrograde motion, and the two refinements (eccentric and equant) \u2014 what each did and why the equant was philosophically troubling. Then explain why the system nonetheless triumphed and endured for fourteen centuries.", "rubric": "THE RETROGRADE MACHINE: the problem was to produce backward loops from UNIFORM CIRCULAR MOTIONS with the Earth AT REST. The solution: a large DEFERENT circle centered near the Earth, a small EPICYCLE whose center rides around the deferent, and the planet riding the epicycle. The planet's motion is the SUM of the two circular motions. When the planet swings through the NEAR side of its epicycle (toward Earth), its epicyclic motion runs OPPOSITE to the deferent's carrying motion, so \u2014 if fast enough \u2014 the planet appears from Earth to move BACKWARD against the stars: a retrograde loop, manufactured entirely from circles. Tuning sizes and speeds reproduced each planet's observed loops. THE ECCENTRIC: Ptolemy offset the EARTH slightly from the deferent's CENTER, so the planet appears faster when nearer and slower when farther \u2014 matching the observed non-uniform speeds. THE EQUANT (the brilliant, controversial device): a special point offset from the center on the opposite side from the Earth, about which the epicycle's center moves at UNIFORM ANGULAR SPEED \u2014 i.e. equal angles in equal times AS SEEN FROM THE EQUANT, neither the center nor the Earth. TROUBLING because: the motion is then uniform only from that arbitrary point, so the planet does NOT truly move uniformly around its circle \u2014 a quiet violation of the CIRCLE DOGMA (genuine uniform circular motion). It fit the data superbly but felt philosophically impure; Copernicus wanted to banish it. WHY IT TRIUMPHED AND ENDURED (~150 AD to ~1543, ~14 centuries, perhaps the longest-lived scientific theory ever): ABOVE ALL BECAUSE IT WORKED \u2014 it PREDICTED planetary, solar, and lunar positions well enough for practical purposes, century after century (a predicting theory is a good theory). It also FIT THE WORLDVIEW: it matched the strong evidence for a stationary Earth and meshed with ARISTOTELIAN PHYSICS (the changeable sublunary realm below the Moon vs. the perfect, eternal, circular-motion celestial realm above), locking astronomy and physics into one coherent, evidence-supported picture. The Almagest itself was comprehensive and rigorously mathematical; only later did its Earth-centered picture become entangled with religious authority. A strong answer emphasizes that its longevity was rooted in genuine empirical success, which is exactly what made overturning it so monumental."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "Ptolemy's system accumulated 'strains' over the centuries. Describe them honestly \u2014 including the two unexplained coincidences and why they are called 'fingerprints of the new system' \u2014 and explain the deeper lesson this chapter teaches about how scientific revolutions actually work. Note also the thousand-year bridge through which the system reached Copernicus.", "rubric": "THE STRAINS (honest picture \u2014 real but not fatal): (1) GROWING COMPLEXITY \u2014 keeping predictions accurate as data improved meant more adjustments and free parameters, eroding elegance and making the system feel like sophisticated curve-fitting rather than deep truth (with the caveat that the 'epicycles piled on epicycles' image is an OVERSTATED caricature; Ptolemy's actual system was intricate but not absurd). (2) THE EQUANT'S DISCOMFORT \u2014 it worked but violated true uniform circular motion, a philosophical blemish. (3) THE UNEXPLAINED COINCIDENCES \u2014 patterns the model reproduced but couldn't explain. THE TWO COINCIDENCES: (i) MERCURY AND VENUS never stray far from the Sun (always morning/evening stars) \u2014 their epicycle centers had to be arbitrarily TIED to the Sun's direction; (ii) RETROGRADE always occurs at OPPOSITION for outer planets \u2014 the epicycles' motions had to be synchronized with the Sun BY HAND. In each case the model could describe but not explain why every planet's motion is chained to the Sun. 'FINGERPRINTS OF THE NEW SYSTEM': both coincidences become AUTOMATIC, INEVITABLE consequences of HELIOCENTRISM \u2014 if planets orbit the Sun, Mercury and Venus (inner orbits) CAN'T appear far from it, and retrograde MUST occur at opposition (when Earth overtakes an outer planet). The facts geocentrism imposed by hand fall out FOR FREE from putting the Sun at the center, so the strains secretly pointed toward the true structure. THE DEEPER LESSON: the honest picture is that the strains did NOT make the system 'obviously wrong' \u2014 it still WORKED and predicted the sky up to the revolution; a reasonable astronomer in 1500 could defensibly call for refinement, not replacement. So the move to heliocentrism was NOT forced by crisis but was a BOLD, RISKY, aesthetically-driven leap most contemporaries found unreasonable (it revived the 'absurd' moving Earth). Scientific revolutions are usually NOT the triumph of obvious truth over foolish error, but the OVERTHROW OF GOOD THEORIES BY BETTER ONES, often against the apparent weight of evidence, driven by a few who glimpse a deeper simplicity. THE BRIDGE: the system reached Copernicus across ~1,400 years and several civilizations \u2014 preserved and ADVANCED through the ISLAMIC GOLDEN AGE (the very name 'Almagest' is Arabic; al-Battani and al-Sufi corrected its numbers; the Maragha school and Ibn al-Shatir built equant-free models using devices like the Tusi couple that reappear almost identically in Copernicus). A strong answer ties it to the course's through-line: the appearances admit MORE THAN ONE geometry that fits, and the deep work of science is discovering which is TRUE \u2014 the ancients found one that worked; the revolution would find the one that was right."}]$astroPT_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/foundations/great-revolution/ptolemy', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
